      "text": "Let the two numbers be $a$ and $b,$ with $a < b.$\nBy the Arithmetic Mean-Geometric Mean Inequality (AM-GM), $\\frac{a + b}{2} \\ge \\sqrt{ab}.$\nSince $\\sqrt{ab} = \\sqrt{3},$ we have $\\frac{a + b}{2} \\ge \\sqrt{3}.$\nSince $a < b,$ we have $\\frac{a + b}{2} = \\frac{3b}{2} \\ge \\sqrt{3}.$\nSquaring both sides, we get $3b^2 \\ge 4.$\nSince $b$ is positive, we have $b \\ge \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}} = \\boxed{\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}}.$\nTherefore, one of the two numbers is $\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}.$\nBy the Arithmetic Mean-Harmonic Mean Inequality (AM-HM), $\\frac{2}{\\frac{1}{a} + \\frac{1}{b}} \\le \\frac{a + b}{2}.$\nSince $\\frac{a + b}{2} = \\frac{3b}{2},$ we have $\\frac{2}{\\frac{1}{a} + \\frac{1}{b}} \\le \\frac{3b}{2}.$\nSince $\\frac{a + b}{2} = \\frac{3b}{2},$ we have $\\frac{2a + 2b}{ab} \\ge 3b.$\nSince $a < b,$ we have $\\frac{a + b}{a} \\ge 3,$ or $a \\ge \\frac{a + b}{3}.$\nSubstituting $b$ for $a + b,$ we get $\\frac{b}{a} \\ge \\frac{3b}{2}.$\nSince $b$ is positive, we have $\\frac{1}{a} \\ge \\frac{3}{2},$ or $a < \\frac{2}{3}.$\nSince $a > 0,$ we have $0 < a \\le \\frac{2}{3}.$\nSince $a < b,$ we have $0 < a < \\frac{2}{3}.$\nTherefore, one of the two numbers is $\\boxed{\\frac{2}{3}}.$",
